        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>In order to show that there is acceptance of vasectomy as a method of birth control by the public and that a large demand goes unmet due to presumed legal restrictions, the unavailability of services, or the unwillingness of physicians to perform the procedure, the Vasectomy Service at the Sanger Bureau was established. The service itself is the main objective; research and training are secondary. After persons making initial inquiries have formally applied and their completed application is processed, an initial interview and a presurgical medical evaluation are conducted. The procedure is explained and the candidate's eligibility determied. The medical evaluation incudes the medical interview, physical examination, semen analysis, blodo studies, and urinalysis. Surgery appointments are scheduled not less than 2 weeks after the medical evaluation. The Bureau requires 2 azoospermic postoperative semen specimens before a letter of confirmation was sent informing the patient that other methods of contraception might be discontinued. The 236 clients seen in the 1st year, October 1969-September 1970, were sent a questionnaire in November 1971. The mean age of the vasectomized men at the time of operation was 38.4; the mean number of living children of acceptors was 3.3 and only 3 men had no children. The men were highly educated and about 58% were in professional, managerial, or technical occupations. About 9% of the men had specific postoperative complications such as small hematoma, bleeding, or infection. About 14% had subjective complaints such as pain, swelling, and discomfort. Results of a follow-up survey 1-2 years later showed that postoperative complications in msot cases were mild and required no specific treatment and little or no absenteeism. It is necessary to streess that male sterility is not accomplished upon completion of surgery and contraceptive measures should be used until azoospermia is confirmed by postoperative semen analysis. Most of the men, 71%, became sterile within 3 months, but 7% continued to show motile sperm in their ejaculates for more than 6 months.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
